-\abstract{Streaming large language model (LLM) services can expose response-length metadata even when payloads are encrypted. We evaluate a fixed T5 implementation with its recorded candidate-selection rule through 6600 reconstructions from validated application-layer length traces. For 300 aligned prompts per model, five archived-reference cohorts yield mean term-frequency cosine scores ($\phi$) of 0.482--0.535; two additional archived cohorts have malformed word boundaries. Newly generated TinyLlama and Phi-3.5-mini responses with verified detokenization yield means of 0.435 and 0.504. Legacy rendering of their saved token IDs reproduces all 600 corresponding archived references and length vectors. Changing both the trace and scoring reference increases mean $\phi$ by 0.356 and 0.383 under matched reconstruction settings. In two-model pilots, three of six partial-ablation contrasts remain significant after Holm correction; neither cross-prompt contrast is conclusive. Five framing transformations reduce frozen-reconstructor mean scores by 36.7--88.7\%. A five-seed study with a local empirical maximum-a-posteriori (MAP) length inverse recovers much of the loss under bucketing and batching. Reconstruction scopes and retained encoder coverage differ across these experiments. The findings quantify lexical behavior of an instrumented pipeline and demonstrate the importance of validating both observations and reference rendering. They do not measure semantic disclosure or demonstrate passive extraction from encrypted transport.}
